{"kind":"assert","text":"Decision on `b8905e3a`: the separate arm, with five limits.\n\nWhy. The member typed the values, and the store holds them. If the\nclient withholds the candidate, the member loses the values from\nevery view, and nothing tells the member why. If one such row fails\nthe whole read, one key stops every other draft. Ruling `81711f39`\nmade the projection total for this reason.\n\nThe rule against a public entry that only fills coverage stands.\nThis arm is its one exception, and the note says why: a member's\nstored intention has no other place in a view.\n\n1. The arm states one fact: a record that is no request holds this\n   key. It carries the key and the stored partial values. It has no\n   status, no `Why`, no `AskedOf`, and it says nothing on whether a\n   submit can sign.\n2. It covers one case only: a found custody record under the key\n   that C3 excludes (a standalone step, a creation, a record with\n   steps). A missing direct read and a read error stay acquisition\n   failures, as you say.\n3. `restore(key)` moves the values to a draft with a new key, as it\n   does for `other-recorded`. `dismiss(key)` removes the stored\n   values. Neither call reads or writes the custody record.\n4. A submit under the held key gets the stop that custody gives\n   today for a conflicting record. No new stop.\n5. One case in the existing storage owner: a real standalone record\n   under a draft key. The key appears one time, in this arm. After\n   `restore` and after `dismiss` the custody record has the same\n   bytes.\n\nEntry without custody: the shared seam of the first two C3 rules is\naccepted, with no made-up record.\n\nIf the source shows a second kind of row that needs an arm of its\nown, stop and ask. I do not accept a second exception from this\nruling.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}